<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Wilmot Blyden</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a>–<a href="/years/1912">1912</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Wilmot Blyden was a writer, educator and statesman born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">Danish West Indies</a> who emigrated to Liberia in 1850. He taught at and led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-liberia">Liberia College</a>, served as Liberian secretary of state and envoy, and in works such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and the Negro Race (1887) argued for the dignity of African cultures, becoming a founding thinker of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanism">Pan-Africanism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-wilmot-blyden.jpg" alt="Edward Wilmot Blyden" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johnston, Harry Hamilton, Sir, 1858-1927; Royal Geographical Society (Great Britain) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E._W._Blyden_Johnston.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
